1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:00,962
TL;DR.

2
00:00:00,962 --> 00:00:03,087
This week in 30 seconds:.

3
00:00:03,087 --> 00:00:16,887
OpenAI: According to the vendor, GPT-6.1 Sol delivers near-Astra performance at one-fifth the price (not yet independently verified), and the new "Dots" agents launch for Pro and Enterprise.

4
00:00:16,887 --> 00:00:25,600
Safety: An OpenAI safety researcher resigns and calls the culture "broken", while the chief research officer promises monitoring during training.

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:34,587
Apple: Because of AI agents, macOS will require more explicit consent for Full Disk Access, which matters for every local agent setup.

6
00:00:34,587 --> 00:00:47,450
Open Source: Holo4 controls desktops with open weights but trails Opus 5.5 clearly on OSWorld 2.0 (61.7% vs. 81.8%).

7
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:48,700
Story of the Week.

8
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:53,137
OpenAI DevDay: Agents With Names, Prices in Free Fall.

9
00:00:53,137 --> 00:00:59,250
Caveat up front: All claims about Sol come from OpenAI's keynote and the live blog covering it .

10
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:04,562
We have no independent benchmarks for Sol yet, so "near-Astra" is a vendor claim.

11
00:01:04,562 --> 00:01:25,150
For one-fifth of the price, OpenAI now offers nearly the intelligence of its flagship, according to the keynote: GPT-6.1 Sol delivers "near-Astra" performance by the vendor's account, but costs only $2.00 input and $10.00 output per 1M tokens, where Astra charges $10.00 and $50.00 .

12
00:01:25,150 --> 00:01:28,937
The cached input price drops from $1.00 to $0.10 .

13
00:01:28,937 --> 00:01:33,437
The one-fifth figure applies to standard speed, not to Ultrafast mode.

14
00:01:33,437 --> 00:01:42,512
The keynote's buzzword was "Dots": customizable agents with their own name and avatar, built on Astra, that carry out tasks autonomously.

15
00:01:42,512 --> 00:01:46,175
Access starts with ChatGPT Pro and Enterprise .

16
00:01:46,175 --> 00:01:49,637
Sam Altman called Astra "our most aligned model" .

17
00:01:49,637 --> 00:01:52,725
That, too, is the vendor's self-assessment.

18
00:01:52,725 --> 00:02:01,662
Speed comes at a price: Ultrafast mode delivers up to 300 tokens per second, 8x the speed, but costs 6x as much.

19
00:02:01,662 --> 00:02:05,675
It is available today for Astra, with Sol to follow soon .

20
00:02:05,675 --> 00:02:11,062
Sol's one-fifth price and the Ultrafast premium can't simply be netted against each other.

21
00:02:11,062 --> 00:02:13,425
The platform business is growing, too.

22
00:02:13,425 --> 00:02:23,862
ChatGPT Sites gets SQLite databases, scheduled tasks, and plugin integration, and the marketplace launches with several partners from design and business software .

23
00:02:23,862 --> 00:02:31,687
According to OpenAI, there are already 8M hosted sites, and 70% of OpenAI employees are building their own .

24
00:02:31,687 --> 00:02:38,275
For teams running agents in production, this means the cost curve is moving faster than the capability curve.

25
00:02:38,275 --> 00:02:48,225
Tasks that could only be justified economically with the most expensive model yesterday can now be trial-switched to Sol, without taking the vendor's claims on faith.

26
00:02:48,225 --> 00:02:51,350
If you use Ultrafast, set a budget limit first.

27
00:02:51,350 --> 00:02:53,012
"our most aligned model"

28
00:02:53,012 --> 00:02:58,187
— Sam Altman, OpenAI CEO, on Astra, the model behind Dots

29
00:02:58,187 --> 00:03:03,925
Open questions: How Sol really compares to Astra can only be judged with independent tests.

30
00:03:03,925 --> 00:03:13,150
And how an autonomous agent with its own name can be secured in day-to-day enterprise use isn't answered by the keynote, which leads us straight to the next story.

31
00:03:13,150 --> 00:03:18,787
Bottom Line: Sol is meant to make agent workloads cheaper, and Dots makes them more personal.

32
00:03:18,787 --> 00:03:22,062
Whether they also get safer is the real question of the week.

33
00:03:22,062 --> 00:03:23,400
More Top Stories.

34
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:26,200
OpenAI: The Fight Over Safety Culture.

35
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:32,712
David Robinson, 3.5 years at OpenAI, has resigned and calls the company's culture "broken" .

36
00:03:32,712 --> 00:03:47,112
His criticism is fundamental: he questions the principle of "iterative deployment" and calls for safety standards like those for nuclear power plants or heavily trafficked airports, along with external incentives rather than internal reforms .

37
00:03:47,112 --> 00:03:50,725
He is targeting the structure, not individual processes.

38
00:03:50,725 --> 00:03:54,650
Meanwhile, Chief Research Officer Mark Chen defends the course.

39
00:03:54,650 --> 00:04:02,687
After the Hugging Face hack, further agent breakouts, and a break-in at the Australian health system, monitoring now runs during training .

40
00:04:02,687 --> 00:04:07,025
Chen admits openly: "We didn't have the monitors on in training before.

41
00:04:07,025 --> 00:04:08,825
It wasn't industry practice.

42
00:04:08,825 --> 00:04:11,787
Now every single thing is put through monitors."

43
00:04:11,787 --> 00:04:17,275
According to the interview, 5-10% of compute was redirected from training to safety.

44
00:04:17,275 --> 00:04:24,062
OpenAI says it detected the September 20 incident within 15 minutes, while Hugging Face needed weeks.

45
00:04:24,062 --> 00:04:28,175
The Australian authorities were informed only after 84 days .

46
00:04:28,274 --> 00:04:36,236
Important context: Chen's commitments and the detection time are OpenAI's own statements and don't answer Robinson's core criticism.

47
00:04:36,236 --> 00:04:48,036
Monitoring during training doesn't change the fact that models continue to be brought to market iteratively, and the late notification of the Australian authorities shows exactly the gap Robinson points to.

48
00:04:48,036 --> 00:04:56,861
So What: If you deploy OpenAI agents in sensitive environments, don't delegate your own monitoring and reporting processes to the vendor.

49
00:04:56,861 --> 00:05:01,724
Claude Sonnet 5.5: Faster, Cheaper, With a Known Bug.

50
00:05:01,724 --> 00:05:05,086
Anthropic has released Claude Sonnet 5.5.

51
00:05:05,086 --> 00:05:08,449
The model now powers the free tier of claude.ai .

52
00:05:08,449 --> 00:05:20,186
Simon Willison describes it this way: "runs 30%+ faster, and costs up to 30% less for most work" and "almost as good as Opus 5.5 on some coding tasks" .

53
00:05:20,186 --> 00:05:23,036
He praises coding and creative generation .

54
00:05:23,036 --> 00:05:30,536
The catch: as with Opus 5.5, at maximum thinking effort the model burns tokens without producing a result.

55
00:05:30,536 --> 00:05:39,674
In the test with "max", 128,000 tokens ($1.28) were consumed and the SVG output failed.

56
00:05:39,674 --> 00:05:45,511
With "xhigh", by contrast, it worked in 41 seconds for 5.74 cents .

57
00:05:45,511 --> 00:05:51,974
So What: Deliberately set effort levels to "xhigh" instead of "max" until the bug is fixed.

58
00:05:51,974 --> 00:05:54,911
Apple Tightens the Reins on Full Disk Access.

59
00:05:54,911 --> 00:06:05,424
Apple is tightening control over macOS Full Disk Access and requiring more explicit consent for broad access to files, messages, mail, and browsing history .

60
00:06:05,424 --> 00:06:11,111
The trigger is reportedly Meta's Muse, which read private messages without explicit permission .

61
00:06:11,111 --> 00:06:14,624
Apple has not yet given concrete implementation details .

62
00:06:14,624 --> 00:06:25,224
Apple justifies the move this way: "As AI agents become increasingly capable and autonomous, the risks associated with this level of access will grow substantially."

63
00:06:25,224 --> 00:06:28,424
Ars Technica also reports on the changed permissions .

64
00:06:28,424 --> 00:06:40,349
For developers, this means terminal apps, coding agents, and local automations that have so far held blanket Full Disk Access may need finer-grained grants or additional confirmations in the future.

65
00:06:40,349 --> 00:06:45,561
What workarounds or exceptions look like is unclear until Apple publishes details.

66
00:06:45,561 --> 00:06:54,811
So What: If you run a terminal or coding agents with Full Disk Access, clean up those grants now and expect restrictions in the next macOS update.

67
00:06:54,811 --> 00:06:55,761
Quick Hits.

68
00:06:55,761 --> 00:06:56,836
Briefly noted:.

69
00:06:56,836 --> 00:07:04,086
Amazon: Is dropping NDAs with authorities on data center projects, according to AWS CEO Matt Garman .

70
00:07:04,086 --> 00:07:16,299
Amazon (numbers): According to its own blog, data centers account for only 0.5% of US industrial water use, and over $1B flowed to communities over three years.

71
00:07:16,299 --> 00:07:18,336
Both are self-reported figures .

72
00:07:18,336 --> 00:07:31,274
Meta: Is releasing Muse gadgets as open source, with firmware and a Linux SDK for Raspberry Pi and ESP32, and is giving away 5,000 Muse Home Link devices to subscribers .

73
00:07:31,274 --> 00:07:42,799
The catch: the same Muse family came under fire at the same time for reading private messages without explicit permission, and is considered one of the triggers for Apple's tightened Full Disk Access rules .

74
00:07:42,799 --> 00:07:43,924
Tool of the Week.

75
00:07:43,924 --> 00:07:48,261
Holo4 (H Company) - Open-weights agents for computer control.

76
00:07:48,261 --> 00:07:57,161
Holo4 controls GUIs, code, APIs, and MCP with a single model, across desktop, web, and Android .

77
00:07:57,161 --> 00:08:08,761
Available are Holo4-27B (dense), Holo4-35B-A3B (MoE), and Holotron4 Nano based on Nemotron 3.

78
00:08:08,761 --> 00:08:18,074
The weights are on Hugging Face (BF16, FP8, NVFP4, 4-bit GGUF), and there is also the H Models API .

79
00:08:18,074 --> 00:08:23,061
H Company aims to "compete with frontier models at a much lower cost per task" .

80
00:08:23,061 --> 00:08:38,611
Putting it in context: on OSWorld 2.0, Holo4 27B reaches 61.7%, the 35B-A3B variant 30.9%, and Opus 5.5 81.8%.

81
00:08:38,611 --> 00:08:43,786
Holo4 thus trails the frontier clearly, but is said to be significantly cheaper .

82
00:08:43,786 --> 00:08:50,011
In the Pac-Man example, it needed 68 calls instead of the comparison model's 197 .

83
00:08:50,011 --> 00:08:53,236
That is a vendor example, not an independent test.

84
00:08:53,236 --> 00:08:58,124
Interesting for teams that want to test computer-use agents locally or on a tight budget.

85
00:08:58,124 --> 00:08:59,874
We have not checked the license.

86
00:08:59,874 --> 00:09:01,836
Holo4 on Hugging Face

87
00:09:01,836 --> 00:09:02,986
Fail of the Week.

88
00:09:02,986 --> 00:09:05,699
"The Lawyer Whose Witnesses Never Existed"

89
00:09:05,810 --> 00:09:15,835
Stephen D. Aarons (New Mexico) filed briefs in a murder appeal citing at least two fabricated witnesses, including Danny and Linda Stanton .

90
00:09:15,835 --> 00:09:29,622
The consequences: direct contempt, exclusion from the court pending a disciplinary investigation, removal from the case, and a $5,000 penalty payable to the State Bar of New Mexico's Client Protection Fund .

91
00:09:29,622 --> 00:09:31,522
A public defender replaces him.

92
00:09:31,522 --> 00:09:39,822
Aarons stated: "I assumed ChatGPT would generate a 'bulletproof summary' but instead it produced wholly fabricated witnesses."

93
00:09:39,822 --> 00:09:47,372
The judge said: "The problem with lawyers relying on AI hallucinations is an above-the-fold story every single day." .

94
00:09:47,372 --> 00:09:57,747
Root Cause: A language model was used as a summarization and research authority, and its output was not checked against primary sources (case file, transcripts).

95
00:09:57,747 --> 00:10:07,672
What we learn: Every person, number, or citation named by an AI gets checked against the original source before it ends up in a document with legal consequences.

96
00:10:07,672 --> 00:10:08,810
Number of the Week.

97
00:10:08,810 --> 00:10:10,122
1/5

98
00:10:10,122 --> 00:10:28,060
That's what GPT-6.1 Sol costs compared to Astra at standard speed, according to the vendor: $10 instead of $50 output per 1M tokens, $2 instead of $10 input, and $0.10 instead of $1.00 cached input, with "near-Astra intelligence" .

99
00:10:28,060 --> 00:10:31,560
Neither price nor performance is independently confirmed.

100
00:10:31,560 --> 00:10:37,285
If you want speed, on the other hand, Ultrafast costs 6x for 8x the speed .

101
00:10:37,285 --> 00:10:41,910
So with Sol the price drops sharply, while speed remains a premium product.

